  "_comment": "Negative. Reproduces the SPARQL-distinctness bypass a reviewer found in the fifth-axis fix: proof:alpha and proof:beta are each declared TWICE under the SAME @id -- every individual declaration is on its own a fully valid ResolverProofRecord (source=PASS per node), but JSON-LD node-merging unifies each pair onto one subject carrying TWO rkaf:sealedResponseArtifact values: one (urn:...:response:shared-bypass) shared between alpha and beta, plus one unique to each (alpha-unique / beta-unique). Before the fix, rkaf:MachineAdjudicationIndependentPairShape's SPARQL bound the unequal unique values, concluded the pair was distinct on the fifth axis, and passed -- even though both proofs also shared one sealed response. The two proofs ARE distinct on the four original axes (issuers, providers, independence groups, model IDs); only the response-artifact cardinality is being abused. Caught now by the sh:maxCount 1 constraint on rkaf:sealedResponseArtifact within the rkaf:machineAdjudicationProof conditional (compiled/shacl/analysis/resolver-proof-record.ttl, generated from constraints/analysis/resolver-proof-record.cue) -- a proof carrying two response artifacts is rejected at the graph layer before the independent-pair SPARQL ever runs, independent of which two values the SPARQL would have bound."
}
